  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-(3)\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of  the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4\\cdot (3) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the  first  quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-12 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
